{
  "_comment": [
    "Brands phishing actually impersonates, with the domains that legitimately send",
    "their mail. The allowlist is the point: a display name claiming to be PayPal is",
    "only suspicious when the sending domain is NOT one of PayPal's, and without this",
    "list the rule fires on every genuine PayPal notification.",
    "",
    "`domains` must be registrable domains, not subdomains - the rules compare",
    "registrable form, so listing mail.paypal.com would be redundant and listing only",
    "a subdomain would make the parent look like an impostor.",
    "",
    "`aliases` are the words a display name or body uses for the brand. Kept separate",
    "from the domain so 'Microsoft 365' and 'Office 365' both map to microsoft.com.",
    "",
    "Deliberately short. Every entry is a maintenance commitment, and a stale",
    "allowlist produces false positives on the brand's real mail - the failure mode",
    "that makes analysts stop trusting a detector."
  ],
  "brands": [
